# Core Idea 💡

Instead of:

```
hash(key)%N
```

Hum banate hain

# Hash Ring

---

# Hash Ring Kya Hai?

Imagine ek circle.

```
0

|

|

|

|

100
```

Actually

```
0 → 2^32 -1
```

Tak values ho sakti hain.

Visualization ke liye:

```

          0

      /       \

   90           10

   |             |

   80           20

      \       /

          50
```

Ye hi ring hai.

---

# Step 1: Servers Ko Ring Pe Rakho

Suppose

```
A = hash(A)=20

B = hash(B)=50

C = hash(C)=80
```

Ring:

```
          0

           |

           |

          20(A)

           |

          50(B)

           |

          80(C)

           |

          100
```

---

# Step 2: Keys Ko Ring Pe Rakho

Example

```
User1 = 25

User2 = 67

User3 = 88
```

---

Ring:

```
20(A)

25(User1)

50(B)

67(User2)

80(C)

88(User3)
```

---

# Rule ⭐

Every key moves CLOCKWISE

until first server found.

---

# Example

User1

```
25

Clockwise

↓

50(B)
```

Store on B.

---

User2

```
67

Clockwise

↓

80(C)
```

Store on C.

---

User3

```
88

Clockwise

↓

Wrap Around

↓

20(A)
```

Store on A.

---

# Why Clockwise?

Bas convention hai.

Some systems anti-clockwise bhi use kar sakte hain.

Important hai consistency.

---

# Visualization 🎯

```
             User3(88)

                   |

                   v

                A(20)

              /      \

             /        \

         C(80)      User1(25)

            |            |

            |            |

          User2(67)    B(50)
```

---

# Biggest Advantage 🚀

Node Add Karke Dekhte Hain

---

Suppose New Server D

Hash:

```
D = 60
```

Ring:

```
20(A)

50(B)

60(D)

80(C)
```

---

# Kaunsa Data Move Hoga?

Sirf

```
50 → 60
```

range ka.

Bas.

---

Example

```
User55

User57

User59
```

Ab D ko milenge.

---

Baaki poora data same rahega.

😎

---

# Compare

Modulo Hashing

```
Server add

↓

Almost ALL data moves
```

---

Consistent Hashing

```
Server add

↓

Only nearby data moves
```

Huge win.

